  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "Full paper text is present. Some figure text is garbled by extraction, but the relevant result tables and prose are readable.",
    "relation": "discusses",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The study compares downstream BPM-task performance across models of different stated sizes and concludes that size alone does not explain performance. It does not measure pretraining loss or run a controlled scaling sweep.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports downstream task metrics by model size, which bear on V6. It does not measure held-out cross-entropy, fit scaling laws, test extrapolation, compare compute-optimal allocations, or hold parameter count fixed while varying architecture. Prompt-template comparisons are not rows for the scaling claim. Results cited from other work are not recorded.",
    "test_reason": "The paper benchmarks seven off-the-shelf models from different families on four BPM tasks and comments that size alone does not explain performance. Size is confounded with family, training data, instruction tuning and quantization: open models are 4–6-bit GGUF quants, and GPT-4 is API-served. GPT-4's '1,760B' is an unofficial estimate. There is no within-family size sweep and no pretraining loss. These cross-model comparisons cannot show whether downstream performance scales predictably, so the size remark is commentary, not a test of V6.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Largely correct. The authors also conclude that GPT-4 is stable but not the best model, and that its cost and latency are not justified for BPM. They note the results do not generalise beyond these models and tasks.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The paper benchmarks task performance across existing models; it does not reproduce or fit the original scaling-law method.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Prompt-template choice changes performance: several models do worse with persona or step-by-step prompts than few-shot prompts, while Falcon2-11b and Claude2-13b benefit from those alternatives. The size-performance pattern also varies across tasks.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Across seven heterogeneous, mostly quantized models (8B–46.7B plus GPT-4), BPM task scores do not rank by stated parameter count. Smaller open models match or beat GPT-4 on some tasks: Claude2-alpaca-13b on activity recommendation (0.861 vs 0.855), Phi3-14b on RPA F1 (0.714 vs 0.671), Mixtral on QA (0.600 vs 0.579).",
        "proposed_claim": "Across model families of differing training recipes, stated parameter count does not predict domain-specific downstream task ranking.",
        "row_ids": [
          "r01",
          "r03",
          "r08",
          "r09",
          "r15",
          "r19"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "controls": "n",
    "capability_check": "y",
    "uncertainty_reported": "n",
    "code_released": "https://github.com/KiriBu10/openLLMinBPM-benchmark",
    "peer_reviewed": "Hawaii International Conference on System Sciences",
    "problems": "The study tests only seven heterogeneous models and four domain-specific tasks, without a controlled size sweep or pretraining-loss measurements. It reports no uncertainty estimates. Models differ in architecture, training and quantization, so their performance differences cannot isolate parameter count. The paper also reports inconsistent Llama 3 size labels and a mislabeled Table 5 caption.",
    "rows_left_out": "Recorded the aggregate task metrics only. Left out category-level RPA precision, recall and F1 (Table 4), QA complexity-level ROUGE-L scores (Table 5), and declarative-constraint-type precision, recall and F1 (Table 6), as these are additional breakdowns of the same task evaluations rather than aggregate outcomes.",
